Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix}$. We want to find the determinant of $A$:
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - \cos^2 x \cot^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{aligned} 
We know that $\cot^2 x = \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x}$ and $\csc^2 x = \frac{1}{\sin^2 x}$. Thus, 
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x \left(\frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x}\right) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*} 
Let $x = \frac{\pi}{4}$. Then $\cos x = \sin x = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so $\sec x = \csc x = \sqrt{2}$. 
Then $\sec^2 x = \csc^2 x = 2$. 
\begin{align*} A &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 1 \\ \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ 1 & \frac{1}{2} & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\ \det(A) &= 2 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ \frac{1}{2} & 1 \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 1 & \frac{1}{2} \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 2 \left(\frac{1}{2} - 2 \cdot \frac{1}{2}\right) - 1 \left(\frac{1}{2} - 2\right) + 1 \left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1\right) \\ &= 2(0) - 1 \left(-\frac{3}{2}\right) + 1 \left(\frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{2}\right) \\ &= 0 + \frac{3}{2} + 1 \left(-\frac{1}{4}\right) \\ &= \frac{3}{2} - \frac{1}{4} \\ &= \frac{6}{4} - \frac{1}{4} \\ &= \frac{5}{4} \end{align*} 
For $x=\frac{\pi}{4}$, the expression evaluates to $\frac{5}{4}$. 

Consider $x = 0$. Then $\sec^2 x = 1$, $\cos^2 x = 1$, $\cot^2 x = 0$, $\csc^2 x = 1$. 
Then $A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$. 
$\det(A) = 1 (1 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 1) - 1 (1 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 1) + 1 (1 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 1) = 1(-1) - 1(-1) + 1(0) = -1 + 1 + 0 = 0$. 
This suggests the answer is 0. 

