Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix}$.
We want to find $\det(A)$.

We can expand the determinant along the first row:
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x (\cot^2 x - \csc^2 x)) - \cos^2 x \cot^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*}
Since $\cot^2 x = \csc^2 x - 1$,
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x (\csc^2 x - 1 - \csc^2 x)) - \cos^2 x (\csc^2 x - 1) + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x (-1)) - \cos^2 x \csc^2 x + \cos^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= -\sec^2 x \cos^2 x - \cos^2 x \csc^2 x + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x \end{align*}
This doesn't seem to lead to anything simple.
Let's expand the determinant along the second row:
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= -\cos^2 x \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ \cot^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + \cos^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - \csc^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= -\cos^2 x ( \cot^4 x - \cot^2 x ) + \cos^2 x ( \sec^2 x \cot^2 x - 1 ) - \csc^2 x ( \sec^2 x \cos^2 x - \cos^2 x ) \\ &= -\cos^2 x \cot^4 x + \cos^2 x \cot^2 x + \cos^2 x \sec^2 x \cot^2 x - \cos^2 x - \csc^2 x \sec^2 x \cos^2 x + \csc^2 x \cos^2 x \end{align*}
It seems like the expansion along the first row is easier, but this approach is also tedious.

Let's expand along the third row:
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= 1 \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \end{vmatrix} - \cos^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \end{vmatrix} + \cot^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 1 (\csc^2 x - \cos^2 x) - \cos^2 x (\sec^2 x \csc^2 x - \cos^2 x) + \cot^2 x (\sec^2 x \cos^2 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \csc^2 x - \cos^2 x - \cos^2 x \sec^2 x \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x + \cot^2 x \sec^2 x \cos^2 x - \cot^2 x \cos^2 x \end{align*}
Still complicated.

Let $x = 0$. Then $\sec^2 x = 1$, $\cos^2 x = 1$, $\sin x = 0$, $\csc^2 x = 1$, $\cot^2 x = 0$.
Thus
\[A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\]
and $\det(A) = 1(0-1) - 1(0-1) + 1(1-1) = -1+1+0 = 0$.

