\begin{enumerate}
    \item The total number of possible outcomes when rolling six 6-sided dice is $6^6 = 46656$. 
    \item We want exactly two dice to show a 1 and exactly two dice to show a 2. This means that the remaining two dice can show any number from 3 to 6.
    \item The number of ways to choose which two dice show a 1 is $\binom{6}{2} = \frac{6!}{2!4!} = \frac{6 \cdot 5}{2 \cdot 1} = 15$.
    \item The number of ways to choose which two dice show a 2 is $\binom{4}{2} = \frac{4!}{2!2!} = \frac{4 \cdot 3}{2 \cdot 1} = 6$.
    \item The number of ways to have the remaining two dice show any number from 3 to 6 is $4^2 = 16$.
    \item Therefore, the number of favorable outcomes is $\binom{6}{2} \cdot \binom{4}{2} \cdot 4^2 = 15 \cdot 6 \cdot 16 = 1440$. 
    \item The probability is $\frac{1440}{46656} = \frac{1440/144}{46656/144} = \frac{10}{324} = \frac{10/2}{324/2} = \frac{5}{162}$.
\end{enumerate}

\boxed{\frac{5}{162}}

