\begin{enumerate}
    \item Let the prime divisors of $n$ be $p$ and $q$.  Then $n = p^a q^b$, where $a, b \ge 1$ are integers. 
    \item The number of divisors of $n$ is $(a+1)(b+1)$. 
    \item We are given that $n^2$ has 27 divisors.
    \item $n^2 = (p^a q^b)^2 = p^{2a} q^{2b}$. 
    \item The number of divisors of $n^2$ is $(2a+1)(2b+1)$.
    \item So, $(2a+1)(2b+1) = 27$.
    \item Since $a, b \ge 1$, we have $2a+1 \ge 3$ and $2b+1 \ge 3$. 
    \item The possible pairs of factors of 27 are $(1, 27)$, $(3, 9)$, $(9, 3)$, $(27, 1)$.
    \item Since $2a+1 \ge 3$ and $2b+1 \ge 3$, we must have $2a+1 = 3$ and $2b+1 = 9$ or $2a+1 = 9$ and $2b+1 = 3$.
    \item If $2a+1 = 3$, then $2a = 2$, so $a = 1$. If $2b+1 = 9$, then $2b = 8$, so $b = 4$.  Then $n = p^1 q^4$.
    \item If $2a+1 = 9$, then $2a = 8$, so $a = 4$. If $2b+1 = 3$, then $2b = 2$, so $b = 1$. Then $n = p^4 q^1$.
    \item In either case, the number of divisors of $n$ is $(a+1)(b+1) = (1+1)(4+1) = 2 \cdot 5 = 10$ or $(4+1)(1+1) = 5 \cdot 2 = 10$. In both cases, the number of divisors of $n$ is 10.
\end{enumerate}
Therefore, $n$ has 10 divisors.
