The volume of a rectangular pool is given by the formula $V = lwh$, where $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the depth. In this case, $l = 12\text{ m}$, $w = 6\text{ m}$, and $h = 4\text{ m}$. 

Since the pool is half full, the volume of water in the pool is half of the total volume. 
The total volume of the pool is $V = lwh = (12\text{ m})(6\text{ m})(4\text{ m}) = 288 \text{ m}^3$. 
Half of the total volume is $\frac{1}{2} \times 288 \text{ m}^3 = 144 \text{ m}^3$. 

Alternatively, we can calculate the volume of water as half the volume of the pool:
Volume of water = $\frac{1}{2} \times (12\text{ m}) \times (6\text{ m}) \times (4\text{ m}) = \frac{1}{2} \times 288\text{ m}^3 = 144\text{ m}^3$.

