\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \sin x, \cos x, \tan x \text{ form a geometric sequence. } \end{align*}
This means that $\cos x / \sin x = \tan x / \cos x$, i.e., $\cos^2 x = \sin x \tan x$.
We have $\tan x = \frac{\sin x}{\cos x}$. Thus,
\[ \cos^2 x = \sin x \cdot \frac{\sin x}{\cos x} = \frac{\sin^2 x}{\cos x} \]
\[ \cos^3 x = \sin^2 x \]
We are looking for $\cos^3 x + \cos^2 x$. Substituting $\cos^3 x = \sin^2 x$, we have
\[ \cos^3 x + \cos^2 x = \sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1 \]
Therefore, $\cos^3 x + \cos^2 x = 1$.

\boxed{1}
