A function $f(x)$ is even if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain.
A function $f(x)$ is odd if $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain.

We want to find the number of functions that are both even and odd.
If a function $f(x)$ is both even and odd, then we must have $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$.
Setting $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$ equal to each other, we get $f(x) = -f(x)$.
This means $2f(x) = 0$, so $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$ in the domain.

Therefore, the only function that is both even and odd is the function $f(x) = 0$.

Thus, there is only 1 function that is both even and odd.

