\begin{align*}
  \label{eq:1}
  a &\equiv 1 \pmod{3} \text{ or } a \equiv 2 \pmod{3} \\
  b &\equiv 1 \pmod{3} \text{ or } b \equiv 2 \pmod{3}
\end{align*} 
Since $a$ and $b$ are not multiples of 3, we have $a \equiv 1 \text{ or } 2 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 1 \text{ or } 2 \pmod{3}$.
We want to find the least possible remainder when $a^2 + b^2$ is divided by 3.
We can consider all possible combinations of $a \pmod{3}$ and $b \pmod{3}$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $a \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 1 + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item $a \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 1 + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item $a \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 1 + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item $a \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 1 + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
\end{enumerate} 
In all cases, $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
Therefore, the least possible remainder when $a^2 + b^2$ is divided by 3 is 2.

